/* PsychLogic — Full-Length Mock 12. Question bank. */
window.MOCK_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "question": "A 5-year-old watches water being poured from a short, wide glass into a tall, narrow glass and insists that there is now &quot;more water.&quot; Which limitation of preoperational thought BEST accounts for this response?", "options": ["Egocentrism", "Centration", "Animistic thinking", "Transductive reasoning"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Centration</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Centration is focusing on one striking feature (the height of the water) and ignoring others (the width). This is why preoperational children fail conservation tasks.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Egocentrism — Egocentrism is difficulty taking another person’s point of view.</li><li>(C) Animistic thinking — Animistic thinking is believing objects are alive.</li><li>(D) Transductive reasoning — Transductive reasoning is linking two particular events as cause and effect.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Irreversibility (cannot mentally reverse the pouring) also explains conservation failure.</li><li>Conservation is gained in the concrete operational stage (about 7–11 years).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a high-frequency area — it has been asked several times in different papers. Keep it at your fingertips."}, {"id": 2, "question": "Which feature BEST distinguishes panic disorder from a single panic attack?", "options": ["An abrupt surge of fear peaking within minutes, with four or more somatic or cognitive symptoms", "Fear of dying during the attack", "At least one unexpected attack followed by a month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour", "Attacks occurring exclusively on exposure to a specific feared object"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>At least one unexpected attack followed by a month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A panic attack can happen in many disorders. Panic disorder needs recurrent unexpected attacks plus at least 1 month of worry about more attacks or a harmful change in behaviour (e.g. avoidance).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) An abrupt surge of fear peaking within minutes, with four or more somatic or cognitive symptoms — This describes any panic attack, not the disorder.</li><li>(B) Fear of dying during the attack — Fear of dying is just one possible symptom of an attack.</li><li>(D) Attacks occurring exclusively on exposure to a specific feared object — Attacks only with a feared object suggest a specific phobia.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>A panic attack needs 4 or more of 13 symptoms, peaking within minutes.</li><li>Agoraphobia is diagnosed separately in DSM-5.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 3, "question": "A 9-year-old begins to complete his homework promptly because doing so ends his mother&#x27;s repeated scolding. His homework completion has increased over the past month. Which principle BEST explains this change?", "options": ["Negative reinforcement", "Positive punishment", "Negative punishment", "Extinction"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Negative reinforcement</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Homework increases because it removes something unpleasant (scolding). Removing an aversive stimulus to increase a behaviour is negative reinforcement.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Positive punishment — Positive punishment adds something unpleasant to reduce a behaviour.</li><li>(C) Negative punishment — Negative punishment removes something pleasant to reduce a behaviour.</li><li>(D) Extinction — Extinction is the fading of a behaviour when reinforcement stops.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Reinforcement always increases behaviour; punishment always decreases it.</li><li>Escape and avoidance learning are forms of negative reinforcement.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This topic keeps coming back in entrance papers, often with a small change in wording. Revise it well."}, {"id": 4, "question": "A trial reports a statistically significant benefit of a new intervention (p = .03). Independent replications repeatedly find no effect, and it later becomes clear that the intervention truly has no effect. The original finding is BEST described as:", "options": ["A Type II error", "Inadequate statistical power", "Construct invalidity", "A Type I error"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Research, Statistics & Ethics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>A Type I error</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A Type I error is rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true — a ‘false positive’. The original trial claimed an effect that does not exist.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) A Type II error — A Type II error is missing a real effect.</li><li>(B) Inadequate statistical power — Low power leads to Type II errors, not false positives.</li><li>(C) Construct invalidity — Construct invalidity is about measuring the wrong concept.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Probability of a Type I error = alpha (e.g. .05).</li><li>Failed replications are central to the ‘replication crisis’.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 5, "question": "In item analysis, a test item has a difficulty index (p) of 0.85 and a negative discrimination index. The MOST likely interpretation is:", "options": ["The item is too difficult and should be retained to challenge examinees", "The item is moderately difficult and discriminates excellently", "The item is very easy and may be miskeyed or flawed, since high scorers are less likely to answer it correctly than low scorers", "The item is very easy but functions well, since it separates high from low scorers"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Assessment & Psychometrics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>The item is very easy and may be miskeyed or flawed, since high scorers are less likely to answer it correctly than low scorers</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> p = 0.85 means 85% got the item right, so it is very easy. A negative discrimination index means low scorers did better than high scorers — a sign the item is flawed or miskeyed.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) The item is too difficult and should be retained to challenge examinees — p = 0.85 means the item is easy, not difficult.</li><li>(B) The item is moderately difficult and discriminates excellently — It is not moderately difficult, and a negative index is poor discrimination.</li><li>(D) The item is very easy but functions well, since it separates high from low scorers — A negative index means the item does not work well.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Best difficulty for discrimination is around p = 0.5.</li><li>Discrimination index D = proportion correct in upper group − lower group.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 6, "question": "Which of the following BEST differentiates an obsession from ruminative thinking in depression?", "options": ["Obsessions are intrusive and ego-dystonic and are often linked to urges to neutralise them, whereas rumination is mood-congruent brooding about the causes and consequences of low mood", "Obsessions are held with delusional conviction, whereas rumination is never distressing", "Obsessions are always followed by compulsions, whereas rumination never is", "Rumination is ego-dystonic, whereas obsessions are ego-syntonic"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Obsessions are intrusive and ego-dystonic and are often linked to urges to neutralise them, whereas rumination is mood-congruent brooding about the causes and consequences of low mood</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Obsessions are intrusive, unwanted (ego-dystonic) thoughts that often bring urges to neutralise them. Depressive rumination is mood-congruent brooding about the causes and effects of feeling low.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Obsessions are held with delusional conviction, whereas rumination is never distressing — Obsessions are not held with delusional conviction, and rumination is distressing.</li><li>(C) Obsessions are always followed by compulsions, whereas rumination never is — Obsessions are not always followed by compulsions.</li><li>(D) Rumination is ego-dystonic, whereas obsessions are ego-syntonic — This reverses the terms: obsessions are ego-dystonic.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Susan Nolen-Hoeksema developed the response styles theory of rumination.</li><li>Insight in OCD can be good, poor or absent.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 7, "question": "A 27-year-old man reports recurrent intrusive thoughts that his hands carry harmful contaminants. He recognises that the fear is excessive. He spends nearly two hours a day washing his hands and avoids public door handles; washing reduces his distress only briefly. Which is the first-line psychological treatment?", "options": ["Thought stopping", "Systematic desensitisation using relaxation as a competing response", "Cognitive restructuring of beliefs alone", "Exposure and response prevention"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Exposure and response prevention</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the first-line psychological treatment for OCD. The person faces contamination cues and does not wash, so anxiety falls without the ritual.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Thought stopping — Thought stopping does not work for obsessions and can make them worse.</li><li>(B) Systematic desensitisation using relaxation as a competing response — Systematic desensitisation is used for phobias, not OCD.</li><li>(C) Cognitive restructuring of beliefs alone — Cognitive work alone is less effective than ERP.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Victor Meyer (1966) first described exposure with response prevention.</li><li>SSRIs are the first-line medicines for OCD.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A favourite of paper-setters — asked again and again in different exams. Expect it in some form."}, {"id": 8, "question": "Which of the following correctly pairs the year with the university associated with the establishment of the EARLIEST department/laboratory of experimental psychology in an Indian university?", "options": ["1905 – Banaras Hindu University", "1915 – University of Calcutta", "1924 – University of Mysore", "1938 – Osmania University"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>1915 – University of Calcutta</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The first department and laboratory of experimental psychology in an Indian university was set up at the University of Calcutta in 1915–16 under N.N. Sen Gupta.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) 1905 – Banaras Hindu University — BHU did not have the first psychology laboratory in 1905.</li><li>(C) 1924 – University of Mysore — Mysore started in 1924, later than Calcutta.</li><li>(D) 1938 – Osmania University — Osmania is not the earliest.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The Indian Psychological Association was founded in 1925.</li><li>Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in 1922.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 9, "question": "Positive emotions are proposed to broaden attention and thought–action repertoires and, over time, to build lasting personal resources such as resilience and social support. This is the central proposition of:", "options": ["Flow theory", "Broaden-and-build theory", "Cognitive dissonance theory", "Opponent-process theory"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Social, Positive & Indian Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Broaden-and-build theory</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Barbara Fredrickson’s broaden-and-build theory (1998) says positive emotions widen attention and thinking and, over time, build lasting resources such as resilience and social bonds.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Flow theory — Flow theory (Csikszentmihalyi) is about deep absorption in challenging tasks.</li><li>(C) Cognitive dissonance theory — Cognitive dissonance theory (Festinger) is about inconsistent beliefs.</li><li>(D) Opponent-process theory — Opponent-process theory (Solomon) explains emotional after-reactions.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Fredrickson also studied the ‘undoing effect’ of positive emotions.</li><li>Seligman founded positive psychology (1998).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 10, "question": "Under Section 115 of the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 (India), a person who attempts suicide is presumed, unless proved otherwise, to be:", "options": ["Suffering from schizophrenia", "Criminally irresponsible only if psychotic symptoms are present", "Liable to involuntary admission for 90 days", "Suffering from severe stress and not liable to punishment"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Suffering from severe stress and not liable to punishment</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Section 115 of MHCA 2017 presumes that a person who attempts suicide is under severe stress unless proved otherwise, and shall not be tried or punished. The government must provide care and rehabilitation.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Suffering from schizophrenia — The law makes no presumption of schizophrenia.</li><li>(B) Criminally irresponsible only if psychotic symptoms are present — Criminal responsibility is not linked to psychotic symptoms here.</li><li>(C) Liable to involuntary admission for 90 days — There is no automatic 90-day admission.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>MHCA 2017 came into force on 29 May 2018.</li><li>Section 18: right to access mental healthcare; Section 5: advance directive.</li></ul><p class=\"ex-why\" style=\"margin-top:14px\"><b>Exam note:</b> Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</p>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 11, "question": "In the Strange Situation, an infant becomes extremely distressed when the mother leaves. On her return, the infant seeks contact but angrily pushes her away and cannot be settled. This pattern is classified as:", "options": ["Insecure–resistant (ambivalent)", "Insecure–avoidant", "Disorganised", "Secure"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Insecure–resistant (ambivalent)</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Insecure–resistant (ambivalent) infants are very upset at separation, then both seek and angrily resist the mother on reunion, and cannot be comforted.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Insecure–avoidant — Avoidant infants ignore the mother on reunion.</li><li>(C) Disorganised — Disorganised infants show contradictory behaviour such as freezing.</li><li>(D) Secure — Secure infants are comforted quickly on reunion.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The Strange Situation was developed by Mary Ainsworth (1970s).</li><li>Resistant attachment is linked to inconsistent caregiving.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 12, "question": "Match the following:", "options": ["1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c, 5-e", "1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a, 5-e", "1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c, 5-e", "1-d, 2-e, 3-a, 4-c, 5-b"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c, 5-e</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> BPRS – broad psychopathology severity; YMRS – mania; Y-BOCS – OCD severity; IDEAS – disability from mental illness (Indian); HAM-D – depression.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) 1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c, 5-e — Option A swaps BPRS and YMRS.</li><li>(B) 1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a, 5-e — Option B swaps Y-BOCS and IDEAS.</li><li>(D) 1-d, 2-e, 3-a, 4-c, 5-b — Option D swaps YMRS and HAM-D.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IDEAS = Indian Disability Evaluation and Assessment Scale, used for disability certification.</li><li>BPRS was developed by Overall and Gorham (1962).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Exams like NIMHANS, CIP and IHBAS keep coming back to this area. Keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 13, "question": "According to Carl Rogers, a child who learns that parental love is available only when she achieves high grades develops anxiety in adulthood whenever she experiences her own wishes to rest. The anxiety arises MOST directly from:", "options": ["Conflict between id impulses and the superego", "Fixation at an early psychosexual stage", "A dysfunctional core schema about failure", "Incongruence between self-concept and organismic experience"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Incongruence between self-concept and organismic experience</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> For Rogers, anxiety comes from incongruence — a gap between the self-concept (shaped by ‘conditions of worth’) and actual organismic experience, such as wanting to rest.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Conflict between id impulses and the superego — Id–superego conflict is Freudian.</li><li>(B) Fixation at an early psychosexual stage — Fixation at psychosexual stages is Freudian.</li><li>(C) A dysfunctional core schema about failure — Core schemas are Beck’s cognitive idea.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Conditions of worth develop when love is conditional.</li><li>Unconditional positive regard in therapy reduces incongruence.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 14, "question": "A researcher compares three treatment groups on anxiety, depression and quality of life, which are moderately correlated outcomes. Why is MANOVA preferred over separate ANOVAs?", "options": ["It takes account of the correlations among dependent variables and limits inflation of Type I error across multiple tests", "It allows several independent variables but only one dependent variable", "It is a non-parametric alternative that needs no distributional assumptions", "It always has more power than a single ANOVA regardless of the correlations"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>It takes account of the correlations among dependent variables and limits inflation of Type I error across multiple tests</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> MANOVA tests several correlated dependent variables together. It uses the correlations among them and protects against inflated Type I error from running many separate ANOVAs.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) It allows several independent variables but only one dependent variable — MANOVA has several dependent variables, not just one.</li><li>(C) It is a non-parametric alternative that needs no distributional assumptions — MANOVA is parametric, with its own assumptions.</li><li>(D) It always has more power than a single ANOVA regardless of the correlations — It does not always have more power.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Wilks’ lambda is a common MANOVA test statistic.</li><li>ANCOVA adds a covariate to ANOVA.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 15, "question": "A 30-year-old man was the driver in a road accident in which his friend died. Since the accident three weeks ago he has had intrusive images of the crash, nightmares, avoidance of vehicles, hypervigilance, and a feeling of being &quot;numb and in a daze.&quot; The most appropriate diagnosis is:", "options": ["Posttraumatic stress disorder", "Acute stress disorder", "Adjustment disorder", "Panic disorder"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Acute stress disorder</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The symptoms began after the trauma and have lasted 3 weeks — between 3 days and 1 month — so this is acute stress disorder. Dissociative symptoms (‘numb and in a daze’) support it.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Posttraumatic stress disorder — PTSD needs symptoms for more than 1 month.</li><li>(C) Adjustment disorder — Adjustment disorder does not need trauma-specific intrusions and is less specific.</li><li>(D) Panic disorder — Panic disorder involves unexpected panic attacks.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>If symptoms continue beyond 1 month, the diagnosis changes to PTSD.</li><li>Trauma-focused CBT can prevent PTSD.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area is asked often. Learn the related points above too."}, {"id": 16, "question": "Rats allowed to explore a maze with no reward for several days show no obvious improvement. When food is later placed in the goal box, they run the maze almost as efficiently as rats rewarded from the start. This is BEST explained by:", "options": ["Insight learning", "Shaping by successive approximations", "Latent learning", "Observational learning"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Latent learning</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Tolman’s latent learning: the rats formed a cognitive map while exploring without reward, but showed it only when food made performance worthwhile.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Insight learning — Insight learning (Köhler) is a sudden solution to a problem.</li><li>(B) Shaping by successive approximations — Shaping rewards closer steps to a target behaviour.</li><li>(D) Observational learning — Observational learning comes from watching others.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Tolman and Honzik (1930) did the classic study.</li><li>It separates learning from performance.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 17, "question": "A screening instrument correctly identifies 90 of 100 individuals with depression and correctly classifies 80 of 100 individuals without depression. Its sensitivity and specificity are, respectively:", "options": ["90% and 80%", "80% and 90%", "90% and 20%", "10% and 80%"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>90% and 80%</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Sensitivity = true positives ÷ all with the condition = 90/100 = 90%. Specificity = true negatives ÷ all without it = 80/100 = 80%.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) 80% and 90% — This reverses sensitivity and specificity.</li><li>(C) 90% and 20% — 20% is the false-positive rate, not specificity.</li><li>(D) 10% and 80% — 10% is the false-negative rate, not sensitivity.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Positive predictive value depends on how common the condition is.</li><li>Screening tests aim for high sensitivity.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a high-frequency area — it has been asked several times in different papers. Keep it at your fingertips."}, {"id": 18, "question": "Arrange the following Eriksonian psychosocial crises in the correct developmental order: i. Initiative vs. Guilt ii. Trust vs. Mistrust iii. Identity vs. Role Confusion iv. Industry vs. Inferiority v. Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt", "options": ["i – ii – v – iv – iii", "ii – i – v – iv – iii", "v – ii – i – iii – iv", "ii – v – i – iv – iii"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>ii – v – i – iv – iii</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Erikson’s order: Trust vs Mistrust → Autonomy vs Shame and Doubt → Initiative vs Guilt → Industry vs Inferiority → Identity vs Role Confusion.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) i – ii – v – iv – iii — Trust comes first, not initiative.</li><li>(B) ii – i – v – iv – iii — Autonomy comes before initiative.</li><li>(C) v – ii – i – iii – iv — Trust comes first and identity comes last of these.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Later stages: intimacy, generativity, integrity.</li><li>Each stage has a virtue (hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This topic keeps coming back in entrance papers, often with a small change in wording. Revise it well."}, {"id": 19, "question": "A man with alcohol dependence says, &quot;I don&#x27;t think my drinking is a problem; everyone I know drinks.&quot; Which therapist response BEST reflects &quot;rolling with resistance&quot; in motivational interviewing?", "options": ["\"You are in denial, and you must accept that you are an alcoholic.\"", "\"Let me explain the liver damage that drinking at your level causes.\"", "\"You're not convinced that drinking is a problem for you, and at the same time something made you come here today.\"", "\"If it is not a problem, why did you come?\""], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>&quot;You&#x27;re not convinced that drinking is a problem for you, and at the same time something made you come here today.&quot;</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Rolling with resistance means not arguing. The therapist reflects the client’s view and the other side of his ambivalence (‘something made you come here’).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) &quot;You are in denial, and you must accept that you are an alcoholic.&quot; — Labelling and confronting increase resistance.</li><li>(B) &quot;Let me explain the liver damage that drinking at your level causes.&quot; — Lecturing on harm (‘righting reflex’) increases resistance.</li><li>(D) &quot;If it is not a problem, why did you come?&quot; — This question is confrontational and sarcastic.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>MI was developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick.</li><li>MI principles: express empathy, develop discrepancy, roll with resistance, support self-efficacy.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 20, "question": "A newly developed anxiety scale shows a Cronbach&#x27;s alpha of .93, but its scores fail to predict clinician-rated anxiety or clinic attendance, which it is meant to relate to. This suggests a problem with:", "options": ["Reliability", "Validity", "Norms", "Standardisation"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Validity</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A high alpha shows the items hang together (good reliability). But failure to predict related criteria shows poor validity. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Reliability — Alpha of .93 shows good reliability.</li><li>(C) Norms — Norms are about comparing scores with a reference group.</li><li>(D) Standardisation — Standardisation is about uniform administration and scoring.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Criterion validity: concurrent and predictive.</li><li>A test can be reliable without being valid, but not valid without being reliable.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A favourite of paper-setters — asked again and again in different exams. Expect it in some form."}, {"id": 21, "question": "A 41-year-old man has been preoccupied for two years with the conviction that his wife is unfaithful. He checks her phone and questions her repeatedly, a pattern that is understandable in the light of a betrayal in his earlier marriage. He admits there is no concrete proof and that he &quot;could be wrong.&quot; There are no other psychotic features. The phenomenon is BEST termed:", "options": ["Delusion of persecution", "Obsession", "Delusional perception", "Overvalued idea"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Overvalued idea</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> An overvalued idea is a strong, emotionally charged belief that can be understood from the person’s history and is held with less than delusional certainty (he admits he could be wrong).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Delusion of persecution — A persecutory delusion involves being harmed, held with fixed conviction.</li><li>(B) Obsession — Obsessions are intrusive and resisted.</li><li>(C) Delusional perception — A delusional perception gives a normal perception a sudden delusional meaning.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Carl Wernicke described overvalued ideas.</li><li>Morbid jealousy can be a delusion (Othello syndrome) or an overvalued idea.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Not asked directly yet, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — it may appear in a new form."}, {"id": 22, "question": "<div class=\"ar-block\"><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Assertion (A)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">Lithium is a first-line mood stabiliser in bipolar disorder.</span></div><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Reason (R)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index and requires serum level monitoring.</span></div></div>", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Both statements are true. Lithium is first-line because it is effective (and lowers suicide risk), not because it needs monitoring. So R does not explain A.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A — A is wrong because R does not explain A.</li><li>(C) A is true, but R is false — C is wrong because R is true.</li><li>(D) A is false, but R is true — D is wrong because A is true.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Usual maintenance level: about 0.6–1.0 mmol/L.</li><li>Toxicity signs: coarse tremor, vomiting, ataxia, confusion.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a likely new area. Learn the key points above."}, {"id": 23, "question": "Which statement BEST distinguishes Vygotsky&#x27;s view of cognitive development from Piaget&#x27;s?", "options": ["For Vygotsky, development is socially mediated and guided instruction within the zone of proximal development can lead development; Piaget emphasised the child's self-directed construction of knowledge, with stage readiness constraining learning", "For Vygotsky, development proceeds through universal, discontinuous stages; Piaget emphasised language as the primary tool of thought", "For Vygotsky, private speech is a sign of egocentrism that disappears; Piaget viewed it as a tool for self-regulation", "For Vygotsky, equilibration is the main mechanism of development; Piaget emphasised scaffolding"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>For Vygotsky, development is socially mediated and guided instruction within the zone of proximal development can lead development; Piaget emphasised the child&#x27;s self-directed construction of knowledge, with stage readiness constraining learning</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Vygotsky saw development as social: learning with a more skilled person inside the zone of proximal development can lead development. Piaget saw the child as a lone scientist, limited by stage readiness.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) For Vygotsky, development proceeds through universal, discontinuous stages; Piaget emphasised language as the primary tool of thought — Universal stages are Piaget’s idea, and language as the main tool of thought is Vygotsky’s.</li><li>(C) For Vygotsky, private speech is a sign of egocentrism that disappears; Piaget viewed it as a tool for self-regulation — This reverses their views on private speech.</li><li>(D) For Vygotsky, equilibration is the main mechanism of development; Piaget emphasised scaffolding — This reverses equilibration (Piaget) and scaffolding (associated with Vygotsky).</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Scaffolding was coined by Wood, Bruner and Ross (1976).</li><li>Vygotsky’s private speech becomes inner speech.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area turns up regularly in papers such as IHBAS and RML. Keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 24, "question": "Which finding is REQUIRED to diagnose autism spectrum disorder rather than social (pragmatic) communication disorder?", "options": ["Delayed onset of spoken language", "Impaired intellectual functioning", "Presence of restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests, or activities", "Deficits in social-emotional reciprocity"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Presence of restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests, or activities</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Restricted, repetitive behaviours, interests or activities are needed for autism. Their absence (with social communication problems) gives social (pragmatic) communication disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Delayed onset of spoken language — Delayed language is not required for either.</li><li>(B) Impaired intellectual functioning — Intellectual impairment is not required for either.</li><li>(D) Deficits in social-emotional reciprocity — Social-emotional reciprocity problems can be present in both.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Social (pragmatic) communication disorder was new in DSM-5.</li><li>Autism needs all 3 social items and at least 2 of 4 RRBs.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 25, "question": "Which of the following correctly distinguishes the Rorschach from the TAT?", "options": ["The Rorschach elicits imaginative stories about interpersonal themes, whereas the TAT is scored for perceptual determinants", "The Rorschach uses ambiguous inkblots and is analysed mainly for perceptual–cognitive organisation (location, determinants, content), whereas the TAT uses pictorial scenes and elicits narratives analysed for needs, press and interpersonal themes", "Both are scored primarily for needs and press using Murray's system", "The TAT has a formal form-quality scoring system, while the Rorschach is interpreted only through narrative themes"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Assessment & Psychometrics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>The Rorschach uses ambiguous inkblots and is analysed mainly for perceptual–cognitive organisation (location, determinants, content), whereas the TAT uses pictorial scenes and elicits narratives analysed for needs, press and interpersonal themes</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Rorschach uses inkblots and is scored for location, determinants and content (perceptual-cognitive organisation). The TAT uses pictures and story themes scored for needs, press and relationships.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) The Rorschach elicits imaginative stories about interpersonal themes, whereas the TAT is scored for perceptual determinants — This reverses the two tests.</li><li>(C) Both are scored primarily for needs and press using Murray&#x27;s system — Only the TAT is scored for needs and press (Murray).</li><li>(D) The TAT has a formal form-quality scoring system, while the Rorschach is interpreted only through narrative themes — Form quality scoring belongs to the Rorschach, not the TAT.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The Rorschach has 10 cards; the TAT has 31 cards including one blank.</li><li>Exner’s Comprehensive System is the main Rorschach scoring system.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area is asked often. Learn the related points above too."}, {"id": 26, "question": "A therapist finds herself repeatedly feeling unusually protective toward one client, who reminds her of her younger brother, and extends this client&#x27;s sessions without noticing. This is BEST described as:", "options": ["Transference", "Resistance", "Acting out by the client", "Countertransference"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & Interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Countertransference</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Countertransference is the therapist’s own emotional reactions to the client, often coming from the therapist’s past — here, a younger brother.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Transference — Transference is the client’s feelings towards the therapist.</li><li>(B) Resistance — Resistance is the client’s opposition to therapy work.</li><li>(C) Acting out by the client — Acting out is the client expressing feelings through action.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Modern views see countertransference as a useful source of information.</li><li>Supervision helps manage countertransference.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 27, "question": "(Case 1) A 24-year-old man, a final-year engineering student, is brought by his brother. The family reports that eight months ago he began withdrawing from friends, stopped attending classes, and his grades fell sharply. For the past five weeks he has believed that classmates are discussing his thoughts and that strangers can &quot;read his mind.&quot; When alone, he hears two voices talking about him in the third person. His mood is described as &quot;flat&quot;; he denies sadness, and there has been no period of elevated mood. Urine drug screen, neurological examination and MRI are normal. Which diagnosis is MOST appropriate?", "options": ["Schizophreniform disorder", "Delusional disorder", "Schizophrenia", "Schizoaffective disorder"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Schizophrenia</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Delusions and hallucinations for 5 weeks plus 8 months of decline (prodrome) give a total of more than 6 months, with negative symptoms, no mood episode and no organic cause — schizophrenia.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Schizophreniform disorder — Schizophreniform disorder lasts less than 6 months in total.</li><li>(B) Delusional disorder — Delusional disorder does not have prominent hallucinations or negative symptoms.</li><li>(D) Schizoaffective disorder — Schizoaffective disorder needs a major mood episode.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Third-person voices and thought broadcasting are first-rank symptoms.</li><li>Duration includes prodromal and residual phases.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a high-frequency area — it has been asked several times in different papers. Keep it at your fingertips."}, {"id": 28, "question": "A student sees an upcoming viva as threatening but judges that she has enough preparation and support to cope with it. In Lazarus and Folkman&#x27;s transactional model, this judgement is:", "options": ["Secondary appraisal", "Primary appraisal", "Reappraisal of the coping outcome", "Stress inoculation"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Secondary appraisal</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Judging that you have enough resources to cope is secondary appraisal. Seeing the viva as a threat was the primary appraisal.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Primary appraisal — Primary appraisal judges whether the event is a threat, harm or challenge.</li><li>(C) Reappraisal of the coping outcome — Reappraisal is updating appraisals later.</li><li>(D) Stress inoculation — Stress inoculation is Meichenbaum’s therapy, not an appraisal.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Lazarus and Folkman, Stress, Appraisal and Coping (1984).</li><li>Coping is problem-focused or emotion-focused.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 29, "question": "A 33-year-old woman has had two episodes of major depression. Her husband recalls a five-day period last year when she slept three hours a night, felt unusually energetic, talked more than usual, and began several projects. She continued to go to work; there was no hospitalisation and no psychotic features. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Bipolar I disorder", "Cyclothymic disorder", "Major depressive disorder with mixed features", "Bipolar II disorder"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Bipolar II disorder</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Major depressive episodes plus a 5-day hypomanic episode (at least 4 days, no marked impairment, no hospital, no psychosis) give bipolar II disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Bipolar I disorder — Bipolar I needs a full manic episode (at least 1 week, or hospitalisation).</li><li>(B) Cyclothymic disorder — Cyclothymia does not include full major depressive episodes.</li><li>(C) Major depressive disorder with mixed features — Mixed features means manic symptoms during the depressive episode, not a separate episode.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Mania: at least 7 days; hypomania: at least 4 days.</li><li>Bipolar II is often first seen as depression.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This topic keeps coming back in entrance papers, often with a small change in wording. Revise it well."}, {"id": 30, "question": "In Baddeley&#x27;s revised working memory model, which component is proposed to integrate information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad and long-term memory into multimodal representations?", "options": ["Central executive", "Episodic buffer", "Phonological loop", "Visuospatial sketchpad"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Core Psychological Processes", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Episodic buffer</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Baddeley (2000) added the episodic buffer to his model. It combines information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad and long-term memory into single episodes.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Central executive — The central executive controls attention but does not store or integrate information itself.</li><li>(C) Phonological loop — The phonological loop holds speech-based information.</li><li>(D) Visuospatial sketchpad — The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The original model was Baddeley and Hitch (1974).</li><li>Working memory capacity is linked to fluid intelligence.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A favourite of paper-setters — asked again and again in different exams. Expect it in some form."}, {"id": 31, "question": "Mischel&#x27;s critique of trait theory mainly argued that:", "options": ["Traits are entirely biological in origin", "Traits predict behaviour better than situations do", "Cross-situational consistency of behaviour is low, so situational factors often outweigh broad traits", "Traits cannot be measured reliably"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Core Psychological Processes", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Cross-situational consistency of behaviour is low, so situational factors often outweigh broad traits</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Walter Mischel (Personality and Assessment, 1968) found that behaviour is not very consistent across situations — trait–behaviour correlations rarely exceed about .30 — so situations matter greatly.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Traits are entirely biological in origin — Mischel did not claim traits are fully biological.</li><li>(B) Traits predict behaviour better than situations do — He argued the opposite: situations often predict better.</li><li>(D) Traits cannot be measured reliably — His main point was low consistency, not poor measurement.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>This started the ‘person–situation debate’.</li><li>His later CAPS model sees behaviour as ‘if–then’ patterns.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 32, "question": "After her mother&#x27;s death, a 22-year-old woman reports that for several weeks she has felt as if she were watching herself from outside her body and that her surroundings look &quot;unreal, like a film set.&quot; She knows that these are changes in how she experiences things and that the world has not actually changed. She has no voices or fixed false beliefs. The experience is BEST described as:", "options": ["Depersonalisation–derealisation with intact reality testing", "Delusion of derealisation", "Passivity phenomenon", "Visual pseudohallucination"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Depersonalisation–derealisation with intact reality testing</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Feeling detached from oneself (depersonalisation) and that the world is unreal (derealisation), while knowing these are only feelings (reality testing intact), is depersonalisation–derealisation.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Delusion of derealisation — A delusion would be a fixed false belief, without insight.</li><li>(C) Passivity phenomenon — Passivity phenomena are feelings of external control.</li><li>(D) Visual pseudohallucination — Pseudohallucinations are perceptions in inner space, not feelings of unreality.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>It can follow severe stress, grief or drug use.</li><li>DSM-5 classes it with the dissociative disorders.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 33, "question": "Match the following:", "options": ["1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d, 5-e", "1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-d, 5-e", "1-c, 2-a, 3-b, 4-d, 5-e", "1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-e, 5-d"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-d, 5-e</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Cronbach’s alpha – internal consistency for continuous or polytomous items; Cohen’s kappa – chance-corrected agreement between raters; KR-20 – internal consistency for dichotomous items; Spearman–Brown – full-length reliability from split-half; test–retest – stability over time.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) 1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d, 5-e — Option A swaps kappa and KR-20.</li><li>(C) 1-c, 2-a, 3-b, 4-d, 5-e — Option C swaps alpha and KR-20.</li><li>(D) 1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-e, 5-d — Option D swaps Spearman–Brown and test–retest.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>KR-20 was developed by Kuder and Richardson (1937).</li><li>Cronbach published alpha in 1951.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 34, "question": "On the Bender–Gestalt test, which finding is MOST suggestive of organic cerebral dysfunction rather than emotional disturbance?", "options": ["Very small, constricted drawings", "Substitution of dashes for circles", "Careless overworking of lines", "Perseveration and rotation of figures"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Assessment & Psychometrics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Perseveration and rotation of figures</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Rotation of designs and perseveration are classic organic signs on the Bender test (Lauretta Bender, 1938).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Very small, constricted drawings — Very small drawings are linked with anxiety or withdrawal.</li><li>(B) Substitution of dashes for circles — Dashes for circles are linked with impulsivity in some scoring systems.</li><li>(C) Careless overworking of lines — Overworked lines are linked with anxiety or aggression.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The Bender test has 9 designs.</li><li>Koppitz developed scoring systems for children.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 35, "question": "A 40-year-old man with depression has withdrawn from most activities and spends the day in bed, saying, &quot;I&#x27;ll do things when I feel better.&quot; Which strategy is MOST consistent with behavioural activation?", "options": ["Monitor activity and mood, then schedule graded, valued activities regardless of current mood", "Interpret the symbolic meaning of his dreams", "Wait until energy returns before scheduling any tasks", "Test the evidence for automatic thoughts using a three-column thought record as the main technique"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Monitor activity and mood, then schedule graded, valued activities regardless of current mood</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Behavioural activation works ‘outside-in’: monitor activity and mood, then schedule graded, meaningful activities whatever the mood. Action comes before motivation.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Interpret the symbolic meaning of his dreams — Dream interpretation is psychoanalytic.</li><li>(C) Wait until energy returns before scheduling any tasks — Waiting to feel better keeps the avoidance going.</li><li>(D) Test the evidence for automatic thoughts using a three-column thought record as the main technique — Thought records are cognitive techniques, not the core of BA.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>BA comes from Lewinsohn’s work; Jacobson’s study (1996) showed its strength.</li><li>Activity scheduling is also part of Beck’s CBT.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 36, "question": "An adolescent is actively exploring several career and value alternatives but has not yet committed to any. In Marcia&#x27;s identity status model this is:", "options": ["Foreclosure", "Diffusion", "Moratorium", "Achievement"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Moratorium</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Moratorium is active exploration of choices without yet committing.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Foreclosure — Foreclosure is commitment without exploration.</li><li>(B) Diffusion — Diffusion is neither exploration nor commitment.</li><li>(D) Achievement — Achievement is commitment after exploration.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>James Marcia (1966) built the four statuses on Erikson’s identity stage.</li><li>Moratorium often comes before achievement.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 37, "question": "<div class=\"ar-block\"><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Assertion (A)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">Negative symptoms of schizophrenia respond robustly to D2-receptor blockade by first-generation antipsychotics.</span></div><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Reason (R)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">Negative symptoms are associated with reduced dopaminergic activity in the mesocortical pathway.</span></div></div>", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>A is false, but R is true</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A is false: first-generation antipsychotics block D2 receptors and help positive symptoms, but have little effect on negative symptoms. R is true: negative symptoms are linked to low dopamine in the mesocortical pathway.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A — A is wrong because A is false.</li><li>(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A — B is wrong because A is false.</li><li>(C) A is true, but R is false — C is wrong because A is false and R is true.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Positive symptoms are linked to excess dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway.</li><li>Blocking the nigrostriatal pathway causes extrapyramidal side effects.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area is asked often. Learn the related points above too."}, {"id": 38, "question": "Which finding would MOST strongly support a genetic explanation for schizophrenia in an adoption study?", "options": ["Higher rates of schizophrenia in adoptees whose adoptive parents had schizophrenia than in adoptees whose biological parents did", "Higher rates of schizophrenia in adoptees whose biological parents had schizophrenia than in adoptees whose biological parents did not, regardless of adoptive-family environment", "Higher rates of schizophrenia in adoptees raised in stressful adoptive homes, regardless of biological parent diagnosis", "No difference in rates between adoptees of affected and non-affected biological parents"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Higher rates of schizophrenia in adoptees whose biological parents had schizophrenia than in adoptees whose biological parents did not, regardless of adoptive-family environment</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Adoption studies separate genes from upbringing. Higher rates in adoptees with an affected biological parent, whatever the adoptive home, point to genetic influence.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Higher rates of schizophrenia in adoptees whose adoptive parents had schizophrenia than in adoptees whose biological parents did — This would point to environment.</li><li>(C) Higher rates of schizophrenia in adoptees raised in stressful adoptive homes, regardless of biological parent diagnosis — This would point to environment.</li><li>(D) No difference in rates between adoptees of affected and non-affected biological parents — No difference would argue against a genetic effect.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Heston (1966) and Kety’s Danish studies are classic examples.</li><li>Twin concordance: about 40–50% in identical twins.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 39, "question": "According to social identity theory, intergroup prejudice arises primarily from:", "options": ["Categorising people into in-groups and out-groups and favouring one's own group to enhance self-esteem", "Competition between groups for scarce resources", "Frustration displaced onto scapegoats", "Social facilitation in crowds"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Social, Positive & Indian Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Categorising people into in-groups and out-groups and favouring one&#x27;s own group to enhance self-esteem</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Tajfel and Turner’s social identity theory says we sort people into in-groups and out-groups and favour our own group to boost self-esteem — even without competition (minimal group studies).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Competition between groups for scarce resources — Competition for resources is realistic conflict theory (Sherif’s Robbers Cave).</li><li>(C) Frustration displaced onto scapegoats — Displaced frustration is scapegoat theory.</li><li>(D) Social facilitation in crowds — Social facilitation is about performance in front of others.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Minimal group paradigm (Tajfel, 1970s).</li><li>Self-categorisation theory (Turner) extended it.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a high-frequency area — it has been asked several times in different papers. Keep it at your fingertips."}, {"id": 40, "question": "Which of the following is NOT one of the three basic psychological needs in self-determination theory?", "options": ["Autonomy", "Competence", "Achievement", "Relatedness"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Achievement</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Deci and Ryan’s self-determination theory names three basic needs: autonomy, competence and relatedness. Achievement is McClelland’s motive.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Autonomy — Autonomy is a basic need in SDT.</li><li>(B) Competence — Competence is a basic need in SDT.</li><li>(D) Relatedness — Relatedness is a basic need in SDT.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>SDT separates intrinsic and extrinsic motivation.</li><li>Rewards can undermine intrinsic motivation (overjustification effect).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 41, "question": "Damasio&#x27;s somatic marker hypothesis proposes that decision-making under uncertainty depends primarily on:", "options": ["The amygdala's role in fear conditioning", "The ventromedial prefrontal cortex linking bodily (emotional) signals with cognitive evaluation of options", "Hypothalamic regulation of autonomic responses", "The hippocampus consolidating declarative memories of past outcomes"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>The ventromedial prefrontal cortex linking bodily (emotional) signals with cognitive evaluation of options</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Antonio Damasio’s somatic marker hypothesis says bodily emotional signals from past outcomes guide decisions. The ventromedial prefrontal cortex links these signals with options.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) The amygdala&#x27;s role in fear conditioning — The amygdala is involved, but the key hub in this theory is the vmPFC.</li><li>(C) Hypothalamic regulation of autonomic responses — The hypothalamus controls autonomic responses but is not the central idea.</li><li>(D) The hippocampus consolidating declarative memories of past outcomes — The hippocampus stores memories but is not the theory’s focus.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Patients with vmPFC damage do poorly on the Iowa Gambling Task.</li><li>Damasio wrote Descartes’ Error (1994).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 42, "question": "Which MMPI-2 validity indicator is LEAST confounded by genuine severe psychopathology when screening for over-reporting of symptoms?", "options": ["F", "L", "K", "Fp"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Fp</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Fp (Infrequency-Psychopathology) scale uses items rarely endorsed even by real psychiatric inpatients, so it is less raised by genuine severe illness than F.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) F — F rises with both genuine severe illness and over-reporting.</li><li>(B) L — L measures naive under-reporting (‘faking good’).</li><li>(C) K — K measures defensiveness.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>MMPI-2 has 567 items and 10 clinical scales.</li><li>VRIN and TRIN check inconsistent responding.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This topic keeps coming back in entrance papers, often with a small change in wording. Revise it well."}, {"id": 43, "question": "Bleuler&#x27;s fundamental symptoms of schizophrenia, the &quot;four A&#x27;s,&quot; are:", "options": ["Affect, Anhedonia, Avolition, Alogia", "Attention, Affect, Ambivalence, Aphasia", "Affect (blunting), Ambivalence, Autism, Association disturbance", "Affect, Amnesia, Autism, Anxiety"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Affect (blunting), Ambivalence, Autism, Association disturbance</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Bleuler’s four A’s (1911): Affect (blunted), Ambivalence, Autism and Associations (loosening).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Affect, Anhedonia, Avolition, Alogia — These are mostly modern negative symptoms.</li><li>(B) Attention, Affect, Ambivalence, Aphasia — Attention and aphasia are not in the four A’s.</li><li>(D) Affect, Amnesia, Autism, Anxiety — Amnesia and anxiety are not in the four A’s.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Bleuler coined ‘schizophrenia’ (1908/1911).</li><li>IHBAS 2025 asked Bleuler’s four A’s.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 44, "question": "Which statement about the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) is correct?", "options": ["It is a statutory body set up under the RCI Act, 1992, to regulate training and maintain a Central Rehabilitation Register of rehabilitation professionals, including clinical psychologists", "It is a research body functioning under the Indian Council of Medical Research", "It is a department of NIMHANS that conducts all M.Phil. entrance examinations", "It was constituted under the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 as the Central Mental Health Authority"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>It is a statutory body set up under the RCI Act, 1992, to regulate training and maintain a Central Rehabilitation Register of rehabilitation professionals, including clinical psychologists</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> RCI is a statutory body under the RCI Act, 1992 (amended 2000). It regulates training and keeps the Central Rehabilitation Register of professionals, including clinical psychologists.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) It is a research body functioning under the Indian Council of Medical Research — RCI is not part of ICMR.</li><li>(C) It is a department of NIMHANS that conducts all M.Phil. entrance examinations — RCI is not a department of NIMHANS.</li><li>(D) It was constituted under the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 as the Central Mental Health Authority — The Central Mental Health Authority comes under MHCA 2017; RCI does not.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Practising without RCI registration is an offence.</li><li>RCI works under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment.</li></ul><p class=\"ex-why\" style=\"margin-top:14px\"><b>Exam note:</b> Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</p>", "examWatch": "A favourite of paper-setters — asked again and again in different exams. Expect it in some form."}, {"id": 45, "question": "Which of the following correctly pairs the year with the institution where Stanley Milgram began his obedience-to-authority experiments?", "options": ["1951 – Swarthmore College", "1958 – University of Wisconsin", "1971 – Stanford University", "1961 – Yale University"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Social, Positive & Indian Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>1961 – Yale University</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Stanley Milgram began his obedience experiments at Yale University in 1961, and published them in 1963.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) 1951 – Swarthmore College — 1951, Swarthmore points to Asch’s conformity studies.</li><li>(B) 1958 – University of Wisconsin — 1958, Wisconsin points to Harlow’s monkey studies.</li><li>(C) 1971 – Stanford University — 1971, Stanford points to Zimbardo’s prison study.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>About 65% of participants went to the maximum 450 volts.</li><li>Milgram’s work raised major ethical questions.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 46, "question": "Which pattern is MOST consistent with healthy cognitive ageing?", "options": ["Earlier and steeper decline in processing speed and fluid reasoning than in crystallised vocabulary knowledge", "Decline in vocabulary preceding decline in processing speed", "Marked loss of procedural memory with preserved episodic memory", "Equal rates of decline across all cognitive abilities"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Earlier and steeper decline in processing speed and fluid reasoning than in crystallised vocabulary knowledge</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> With healthy ageing, processing speed and fluid reasoning decline earlier and faster, while crystallised knowledge such as vocabulary stays stable or grows.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Decline in vocabulary preceding decline in processing speed — Vocabulary is among the last abilities to decline.</li><li>(C) Marked loss of procedural memory with preserved episodic memory — Procedural memory is relatively well kept with age.</li><li>(D) Equal rates of decline across all cognitive abilities — Different abilities decline at different rates.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Salthouse linked ageing mainly to slower processing speed.</li><li>Horn and Cattell’s Gf–Gc theory.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 47, "question": "Match the following disorders of thought form with their descriptions:", "options": ["1-d, 2-c, 3-e, 4-a, 5-b", "1-c, 2-d, 3-e, 4-a, 5-b", "1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-e, 5-b", "1-c, 2-e, 3-d, 4-a, 5-b"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>1-c, 2-d, 3-e, 4-a, 5-b</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Derailment – slipping between unrelated topics; tangentiality – oblique replies that never reach the point; circumstantiality – too much detail but gets there; thought blocking – sudden stop; clang association – words linked by sound.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) 1-d, 2-c, 3-e, 4-a, 5-b — Option A swaps derailment and tangentiality.</li><li>(C) 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-e, 5-b — Option C swaps circumstantiality and thought blocking.</li><li>(D) 1-c, 2-e, 3-d, 4-a, 5-b — Option D swaps tangentiality and circumstantiality.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Andreasen’s TLC scale rates formal thought disorder.</li><li>Flight of ideas is typical of mania.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 48, "question": "A researcher hypothesises that the effect of life stress on depressive symptoms is stronger among individuals with low social support than among those with high social support. The most appropriate analysis is:", "options": ["Mediation analysis", "Confirmatory factor analysis", "Moderation analysis (interaction term)", "Partial correlation"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Moderation analysis (interaction term)</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> When the effect of X (stress) on Y (depression) changes with the level of a third variable (social support), that variable is a moderator. It is tested with an interaction term.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Mediation analysis — Mediation explains how X leads to Y through another variable.</li><li>(B) Confirmatory factor analysis — CFA tests a factor structure.</li><li>(D) Partial correlation — Partial correlation removes a third variable’s effect.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Baron and Kenny (1986) separated moderators and mediators.</li><li>Stress-buffering hypothesis (Cohen and Wills, 1985).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 49, "question": "A 45-year-old man admitted with a femur fracture becomes agitated on day 3. He is disoriented to time and place, with fluctuating alertness. He describes vivid visions of insects crawling on the bed and has a coarse tremor, tachycardia, sweating and a temperature of 38.2°C. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Alcohol withdrawal delirium", "Alcoholic hallucinosis", "Wernicke's encephalopathy", "Korsakoff syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Social, Positive & Indian Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Alcohol withdrawal delirium</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Delirium tremens: 2–4 days after stopping alcohol, with disorientation, fluctuating consciousness, vivid visual hallucinations (often insects), tremor and autonomic overactivity (fever, fast pulse, sweating).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Alcoholic hallucinosis — Alcoholic hallucinosis has hallucinations with a clear sensorium.</li><li>(C) Wernicke&#x27;s encephalopathy — Wernicke’s encephalopathy has confusion, ophthalmoplegia and ataxia.</li><li>(D) Korsakoff syndrome — Korsakoff syndrome is chronic amnesia with confabulation.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DT is a medical emergency; benzodiazepines are the main treatment.</li><li>Thiamine is given to prevent Wernicke’s encephalopathy.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A fresh, predicted question. The neighbouring topics are asked often, so it is worth knowing."}, {"id": 50, "question": "Which theorist–concept pair is correctly matched?", "options": ["Adler – collective unconscious", "Jung – inferiority complex", "Maslow – conditions of worth", "Kelly – constructive alternativism"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Kelly – constructive alternativism</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> George Kelly’s personal construct theory rests on constructive alternativism: any event can be interpreted (construed) in other ways.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Adler – collective unconscious — The collective unconscious is Jung’s.</li><li>(B) Jung – inferiority complex — The inferiority complex is Adler’s.</li><li>(C) Maslow – conditions of worth — Conditions of worth are Rogers’.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Kelly developed the Role Construct Repertory (Rep) Test.</li><li>He saw people as ‘naive scientists’.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 51, "question": "Which intervention is MOST characteristic of structural family therapy?", "options": ["Constructing a genogram to trace multigenerational emotional patterns", "Prescribing the symptom paradoxically", "Using enactment and boundary-making to realign family subsystems", "Externalising the problem through re-authoring narratives"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & Interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Using enactment and boundary-making to realign family subsystems</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Salvador Minuchin’s structural family therapy uses enactment (acting out family patterns in session) and boundary-making to reorganise subsystems.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Constructing a genogram to trace multigenerational emotional patterns — Genograms belong to Bowen’s family systems therapy.</li><li>(B) Prescribing the symptom paradoxically — Prescribing the symptom belongs to strategic therapy (Haley, MRI).</li><li>(D) Externalising the problem through re-authoring narratives — Externalising the problem belongs to narrative therapy (White and Epston).</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Minuchin described enmeshed and disengaged families.</li><li>His book: Families and Family Therapy (1974).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 52, "question": "(Case 2) A 9-year-old boy is referred for poor academic performance, daydreaming in class, and frequent loss of belongings. On the WISC-IV he obtains: Verbal Comprehension Index 106, Perceptual Reasoning Index 104, Working Memory Index 79, Processing Speed Index 77, Full Scale IQ 92. Which interpretation is MOST defensible?", "options": ["The Full Scale IQ of 92 shows average ability, so no further evaluation is required", "The marked index scatter limits the usefulness of the Full Scale IQ; the relative weaknesses in working memory and processing speed warrant evaluation for attention/executive difficulties and learning problems", "The low Working Memory and Processing Speed indices establish intellectual disability", "The profile confirms a specific learning disorder in reading"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>The marked index scatter limits the usefulness of the Full Scale IQ; the relative weaknesses in working memory and processing speed warrant evaluation for attention/executive difficulties and learning problems</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The large gap between strong verbal and perceptual indices and weak working memory and processing speed (about 27 points) makes the Full Scale IQ a poor summary. This pattern suggests checking for attention, executive and learning problems.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) The Full Scale IQ of 92 shows average ability, so no further evaluation is required — Average FSIQ hides important weaknesses.</li><li>(C) The low Working Memory and Processing Speed indices establish intellectual disability — Intellectual disability needs low overall IQ (about 70) and adaptive deficits.</li><li>(D) The profile confirms a specific learning disorder in reading — The profile alone cannot confirm a reading disorder.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The working memory and processing speed indices make up the ‘Cognitive Proficiency Index’.</li><li>Low WMI and PSI are common in ADHD.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area is asked often. Learn the related points above too."}, {"id": 53, "question": "A 38-year-old woman has had almost no bodily symptoms but for nine months has been convinced that she has a serious cancer. She repeatedly checks her breasts, seeks reassurance, and continues to worry after several normal investigations. She accepts that doctors &quot;may be right&quot; but cannot stop worrying. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Somatic symptom disorder", "Functional neurological symptom disorder", "Delusional disorder, somatic type", "Illness anxiety disorder"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Illness anxiety disorder</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Strong fear of having a serious illness, with few or no bodily symptoms, checking and reassurance-seeking for at least 6 months, is illness anxiety disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Somatic symptom disorder — Somatic symptom disorder needs distressing bodily symptoms.</li><li>(B) Functional neurological symptom disorder — Functional neurological disorder involves motor or sensory symptoms.</li><li>(C) Delusional disorder, somatic type — She accepts she may be wrong, so it is not a delusion.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 replaced hypochondriasis with SSD and illness anxiety disorder.</li><li>Types: care-seeking and care-avoidant.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 54, "question": "Which procedure BEST reduces criterion variance in psychiatric diagnosis?", "options": ["Using a structured diagnostic interview such as the SCID", "Unstructured interviews by senior clinicians", "A battery of projective tests", "A self-report symptom checklist alone"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Research, Statistics & Ethics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Using a structured diagnostic interview such as the SCID</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Criterion variance comes from clinicians using different rules to decide a diagnosis. Structured interviews such as the SCID use the same questions and decision rules, reducing it.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Unstructured interviews by senior clinicians — Unstructured interviews increase variation.</li><li>(C) A battery of projective tests — Projective tests are not diagnostic interviews.</li><li>(D) A self-report symptom checklist alone — Self-report checklists alone do not apply diagnostic criteria.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Other sources of unreliability: information, observation and interpretation variance.</li><li>Feighner criteria (1972) aimed to improve reliability.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a high-frequency area — it has been asked several times in different papers. Keep it at your fingertips."}, {"id": 55, "question": "Bordin&#x27;s model of the working alliance comprises:", "options": ["Empathy, warmth and genuineness", "Transference, resistance and insight", "Agreement on goals, agreement on tasks, and a therapeutic bond", "Rapport, rationale and relaxation"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Agreement on goals, agreement on tasks, and a therapeutic bond</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Edward Bordin (1979) defined the working alliance as agreement on goals, agreement on tasks, and the bond between therapist and client.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Empathy, warmth and genuineness — Empathy, warmth and genuineness are Rogers’ core conditions.</li><li>(B) Transference, resistance and insight — Transference, resistance and insight are psychoanalytic concepts.</li><li>(D) Rapport, rationale and relaxation — This is not Bordin’s model.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The alliance is one of the strongest predictors of therapy outcome.</li><li>Working Alliance Inventory (Horvath and Greenberg).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This angle is new, but the topic around it is popular with examiners — don’t skip it."}, {"id": 56, "question": "Non-suppression of cortisol on the dexamethasone suppression test in melancholic depression is BEST interpreted as reflecting:", "options": ["Enhanced glucocorticoid negative feedback", "Impaired glucocorticoid negative feedback and HPA-axis hyperactivity", "Hypocortisolism due to adrenal exhaustion", "Reduced CRH drive from the hypothalamus"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Impaired glucocorticoid negative feedback and HPA-axis hyperactivity</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Normally, dexamethasone suppresses cortisol through negative feedback. In melancholic depression, cortisol is not suppressed, showing weak feedback and an overactive HPA axis.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Enhanced glucocorticoid negative feedback — Feedback is weakened, not enhanced.</li><li>(C) Hypocortisolism due to adrenal exhaustion — Cortisol is high, not low.</li><li>(D) Reduced CRH drive from the hypothalamus — CRH drive is increased, not reduced.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The DST was studied by Carroll (1981).</li><li>High cortisol may damage the hippocampus.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This topic keeps coming back in entrance papers, often with a small change in wording. Revise it well."}, {"id": 57, "question": "In DSM-5, catatonia is classified as:", "options": ["A specifier applicable to several psychiatric and medical conditions", "A stand-alone disorder found only in schizophrenia", "A subtype of bipolar disorder only", "A personality disorder"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>A specifier applicable to several psychiatric and medical conditions</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In DSM-5, catatonia is a specifier (‘catatonia associated with another mental disorder’) that can occur with schizophrenia, mood disorders and medical conditions. It also appears as catatonic disorder due to another medical condition and unspecified catatonia.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) A stand-alone disorder found only in schizophrenia — Catatonia is not limited to schizophrenia.</li><li>(C) A subtype of bipolar disorder only — It is not a subtype of bipolar disorder only.</li><li>(D) A personality disorder — It is not a personality disorder.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Diagnosis needs at least 3 of 12 signs.</li><li>Lorazepam and ECT are the main treatments.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 58, "question": "A patient started on haloperidol five days ago develops lead-pipe rigidity, a temperature of 39.5°C, labile blood pressure, altered consciousness and raised creatine phosphokinase. There is no clonus or hyperreflexia, and the onset was gradual over two days. The most likely condition is:", "options": ["Serotonin syndrome", "Malignant hyperthermia", "Acute dystonic reaction", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Neuroleptic malignant syndrome: after an antipsychotic, lead-pipe rigidity, high fever, unstable blood pressure, confusion and raised CPK, developing over days.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Serotonin syndrome — Serotonin syndrome has clonus, hyperreflexia and faster onset after serotonergic drugs.</li><li>(B) Malignant hyperthermia — Malignant hyperthermia follows anaesthetic drugs.</li><li>(C) Acute dystonic reaction — Acute dystonia is sudden muscle spasm without fever.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Treatment: stop the drug, cooling, dantrolene or bromocriptine.</li><li>High-potency drugs such as haloperidol carry higher risk.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Not asked directly yet, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — it may appear in a new form."}, {"id": 59, "question": "Which result indicates GOOD model fit in a confirmatory factor analysis?", "options": ["A statistically significant chi-square in a large sample", "CFI above .95 and RMSEA below about .06", "Eigenvalues above 1 for all factors", "Cronbach's alpha above .70 for each factor"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>CFI above .95 and RMSEA below about .06</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Good CFA fit: CFI above about .95 and RMSEA below about .06 (Hu and Bentler, 1999).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) A statistically significant chi-square in a large sample — A significant chi-square suggests misfit, and in large samples it is almost always significant.</li><li>(C) Eigenvalues above 1 for all factors — Eigenvalues above 1 is a rule for exploratory factor analysis.</li><li>(D) Cronbach&#x27;s alpha above .70 for each factor — Alpha is reliability, not model fit.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Other fit indices: TLI, SRMR.</li><li>CFA tests a model defined in advance; EFA explores structure.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 60, "question": "Which event ensures the unidirectional propagation of an action potential along an axon?", "options": ["Saltatory conduction between the nodes of Ranvier", "The relative refractory period caused by hyperpolarisation", "The absolute refractory period caused by inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels", "Temporal summation at the axon hillock"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>The absolute refractory period caused by inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Behind the impulse, voltage-gated sodium channels are inactivated (absolute refractory period), so the action potential cannot travel backwards.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Saltatory conduction between the nodes of Ranvier — Saltatory conduction increases speed, not direction.</li><li>(B) The relative refractory period caused by hyperpolarisation — The relative refractory period limits firing rate but does not set direction.</li><li>(D) Temporal summation at the axon hillock — Temporal summation decides whether firing starts.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>During the absolute refractory period no stimulus can trigger another spike.</li><li>Myelin and axon diameter determine conduction speed.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A favourite of paper-setters — asked again and again in different exams. Expect it in some form."}, {"id": 61, "question": "Match the technique/concept with the therapeutic approach:", "options": ["1-c, 2-e, 3-b, 4-d, 5-a", "1-c, 2-e, 3-d, 4-b, 5-a", "1-e, 2-c, 3-b, 4-d, 5-a", "1-c, 2-b, 3-e, 4-d, 5-a"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>1-c, 2-e, 3-b, 4-d, 5-a</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Wise mind – DBT; downward arrow – Beck’s cognitive therapy; empty chair – Gestalt therapy; unconditional positive regard – person-centred therapy; paradoxical intention – logotherapy.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) 1-c, 2-e, 3-d, 4-b, 5-a — Option B swaps empty chair and unconditional positive regard.</li><li>(C) 1-e, 2-c, 3-b, 4-d, 5-a — Option C swaps wise mind and downward arrow.</li><li>(D) 1-c, 2-b, 3-e, 4-d, 5-a — Option D swaps downward arrow and empty chair.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Empty chair was developed by Fritz Perls.</li><li>Frankl’s other technique: de-reflection.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 62, "question": "Which of the following is an INDIAN tool used to assess the severity and disability of children with autism?", "options": ["Childhood Autism Rating Scale (CARS)", "Children's Global Assessment Scale (CGAS)", "Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism (ISAA)", "Child Behavior Checklist (CBCL)"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism (ISAA)</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ISAA (Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism) was developed in India by the National Institute for the Mentally Handicapped (now NIEPID), Secunderabad. It rates severity and is used for disability certification.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Childhood Autism Rating Scale (CARS) — CARS was developed in the USA (Schopler and colleagues).</li><li>(B) Children&#x27;s Global Assessment Scale (CGAS) — CGAS is a general functioning scale from the USA.</li><li>(D) Child Behavior Checklist (CBCL) — CBCL (Achenbach) is a US behaviour checklist.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ISAA has 40 items in 6 domains; scores above 70 suggest autism.</li><li>IHBAS 2024 asked exactly this.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 63, "question": "A 29-year-old man lives alone, has never sought close relationships, finds little pleasure in most activities, appears emotionally cold, and is indifferent to praise or criticism. He has no odd beliefs, no unusual speech, and no restricted interests, and he performs well at his job. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Avoidant personality disorder", "Schizotypal personality disorder", "Autism spectrum disorder", "Schizoid personality disorder"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Social, Positive & Indian Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Schizoid personality disorder</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Detachment from relationships, little pleasure, emotional coldness and indifference to praise or criticism, with no odd beliefs, is schizoid personality disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Avoidant personality disorder — Avoidant individuals want relationships but fear rejection.</li><li>(B) Schizotypal personality disorder — Schizotypal individuals have odd beliefs and eccentric behaviour.</li><li>(C) Autism spectrum disorder — Autism needs early social communication problems and restricted interests.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Schizoid PD is in Cluster A.</li><li>ICD-11 replaced specific personality disorder types with severity levels and trait domains.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 64, "question": "A 3-year-old says &quot;goed&quot; and &quot;foots.&quot; This is BEST explained by:", "options": ["Imitation of adult speech", "Overregularisation reflecting acquisition of rules", "Reinforcement of incorrect forms by parents", "Fast mapping"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Overregularisation reflecting acquisition of rules</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Errors like ‘goed’ and ‘foots’ show children applying grammar rules (past tense -ed, plural -s) too widely. They have not heard these forms, so this is rule learning, not imitation.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Imitation of adult speech — Adults do not say ‘goed’, so it is not imitation.</li><li>(C) Reinforcement of incorrect forms by parents — Parents rarely reinforce grammatical errors.</li><li>(D) Fast mapping — Fast mapping is quickly learning a new word’s meaning.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>This supports Chomsky’s view of rule-based language learning.</li><li>Children often say ‘went’ correctly first, then ‘goed’ (U-shaped development).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 65, "question": "A researcher wants to increase statistical power WITHOUT increasing the sample size. The most defensible strategy is:", "options": ["Reduce error variance, for example by using a more reliable measure or a within-subjects design", "Lower alpha to .001", "Switch from a one-tailed to a two-tailed test", "Choose an intervention expected to produce a smaller effect"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Research, Statistics & Ethics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Reduce error variance, for example by using a more reliable measure or a within-subjects design</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Power rises when error variance falls. A more reliable measure or a within-subjects design reduces error without more participants.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Lower alpha to .001 — Lowering alpha reduces power.</li><li>(C) Switch from a one-tailed to a two-tailed test — A two-tailed test has less power than a justified one-tailed test.</li><li>(D) Choose an intervention expected to produce a smaller effect — Smaller effects reduce power.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Power = 1 − β; 0.80 is the usual target.</li><li>Power depends on alpha, effect size, sample size and error variance.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 66, "question": "Which statement correctly differentiates the worry of generalised anxiety disorder from the obsessions of OCD?", "options": ["Worries are intrusive images, usually unrealistic, and are followed by neutralising rituals", "Obsessions concern realistic everyday matters and are ego-syntonic", "Worries and obsessions are identical in content and differ only in duration", "Worries typically concern realistic everyday concerns and are not accompanied by ritualised neutralising, whereas obsessions are intrusive, often unrealistic and ego-dystonic"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Worries typically concern realistic everyday concerns and are not accompanied by ritualised neutralising, whereas obsessions are intrusive, often unrealistic and ego-dystonic</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> GAD worries are about realistic everyday matters (money, health, work) and are not followed by rituals. Obsessions are intrusive, often unrealistic, ego-dystonic and lead to neutralising.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Worries are intrusive images, usually unrealistic, and are followed by neutralising rituals — This describes obsessions, not worries.</li><li>(B) Obsessions concern realistic everyday matters and are ego-syntonic — Obsessions are not realistic everyday concerns, and they are ego-dystonic.</li><li>(C) Worries and obsessions are identical in content and differ only in duration — They differ in content and form, not just in duration.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>GAD needs at least 6 months of excessive worry.</li><li>Borkovec saw worry as a form of cognitive avoidance.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 67, "question": "In Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, cognitive defusion aims to:", "options": ["Dispute the logical validity of distressing thoughts", "Reduce the frequency of distressing thoughts through thought stopping", "Change the person's relationship to thoughts, seeing them as thoughts rather than literal truths", "Replace negative thoughts with positive affirmations"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & Interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Change the person&#x27;s relationship to thoughts, seeing them as thoughts rather than literal truths</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Cognitive defusion in ACT changes how a person relates to thoughts — seeing them as words and pictures passing through the mind, not literal truths. Their content is not disputed.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Dispute the logical validity of distressing thoughts — Disputing thoughts is cognitive restructuring (CBT/REBT).</li><li>(B) Reduce the frequency of distressing thoughts through thought stopping — Thought stopping is not part of ACT.</li><li>(D) Replace negative thoughts with positive affirmations — Positive affirmations are not ACT.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ACT was developed by Steven Hayes.</li><li>Defusion exercise: repeat a word until it loses meaning (Titchener’s technique).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 68, "question": "Which test is MOST sensitive for detecting subtle frontal-lobe executive dysfunction in early neurodegenerative conditions?", "options": ["Benton Visual Retention Test", "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test", "Rey–Osterrieth Complex Figure Test", "Finger Tapping Test"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Wisconsin Card Sorting Test</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The WCST is sensitive to frontal executive dysfunction because it needs abstraction, set-shifting and using feedback. Perseverative errors are the key sign.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Benton Visual Retention Test — The BVRT tests visual memory and visuoconstruction.</li><li>(C) Rey–Osterrieth Complex Figure Test — The ROCF tests visuoconstruction and visual memory.</li><li>(D) Finger Tapping Test — Finger tapping tests motor speed.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IMHANS 2025 asked this question.</li><li>WCST was developed by Grant and Berg (1948).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 69, "question": "(Case 3) A 29-year-old nurse has attended the emergency department three times in two months after superficial cuts to her forearms. She says she &quot;cannot bear being alone,&quot; has intense angry outbursts followed by pleading with her partner, and reports mood changes that shift within hours. She has had two prior overdoses. When the therapist ran late to one session, she left and texted, &quot;I&#x27;ll end it tonight.&quot; A structured, skills-based outpatient programme is planned. In the FIRST stage of treatment, which should be the primary target?", "options": ["Processing past traumatic memories through prolonged exposure", "Missed or interrupted therapy sessions", "Improving relationship quality and career goals", "Suicidal and self-harming behaviours"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & Interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Suicidal and self-harming behaviours</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In DBT Stage 1, the order of targets is: life-threatening behaviours first, then therapy-interfering behaviours, then quality-of-life problems. Self-harm and suicidal behaviour come first.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Processing past traumatic memories through prolonged exposure — Trauma processing comes later (Stage 2), after stability.</li><li>(B) Missed or interrupted therapy sessions — Therapy-interfering behaviour is the second target.</li><li>(C) Improving relationship quality and career goals — Quality-of-life goals come third.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DBT has four modes: individual therapy, skills group, phone coaching, consultation team.</li><li>DBT was developed by Marsha Linehan.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 70, "question": "In the WAIS-IV, which subtests contribute to the Working Memory Index?", "options": ["Digit Span and Arithmetic", "Digit Span and Coding", "Similarities and Vocabulary", "Block Design and Matrix Reasoning"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Assessment & Psychometrics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Digit Span and Arithmetic</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> WAIS-IV Working Memory Index = Digit Span + Arithmetic (Letter–Number Sequencing is supplementary).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Digit Span and Coding — Coding belongs to the Processing Speed Index.</li><li>(C) Similarities and Vocabulary — Similarities and Vocabulary belong to Verbal Comprehension.</li><li>(D) Block Design and Matrix Reasoning — Block Design and Matrix Reasoning belong to Perceptual Reasoning.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>WAIS-IV has four indices: VCI, PRI, WMI, PSI.</li><li>WAIS-5 (2024) splits perceptual reasoning into visual spatial and fluid reasoning.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area is asked often. Learn the related points above too."}, {"id": 71, "question": "A researcher hypothesises that childhood adversity leads to emotion dysregulation, which leads to rumination, which in turn leads to depressive symptoms, and that the first link is stronger among individuals with low perceived social support. Which model BEST represents this hypothesis?", "options": ["Parallel multiple mediation", "Moderated serial mediation", "Simple mediation with covariates", "Two-way factorial ANOVA"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Moderated serial mediation</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Two mediators in a chain (dysregulation → rumination) make serial mediation. A moderator (social support) acting on the first link makes it moderated serial mediation.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Parallel multiple mediation — Parallel mediation has mediators side by side, not in a chain.</li><li>(C) Simple mediation with covariates — Simple mediation has only one mediator.</li><li>(D) Two-way factorial ANOVA — Factorial ANOVA tests group differences, not pathways.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Hayes’ PROCESS macro tests such models.</li><li>Moderated mediation is also called conditional indirect effect.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a high-frequency area — it has been asked several times in different papers. Keep it at your fingertips."}, {"id": 72, "question": "A 19-year-old woman wakes with sudden inability to move her right leg after an argument with her parents. Reflexes are normal and there is no atrophy. Which finding would BEST support a functional (conversion) mechanism?", "options": ["Babinski response", "Hyper-reflexia", "Positive Hoover's sign", "Fasciculations"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Positive Hoover&#x27;s sign</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Hoover’s sign: when the person pushes down with the ‘good’ leg, the ‘weak’ leg extends involuntarily — showing it can move. This supports a functional (conversion) cause.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Babinski response — A Babinski response suggests organic upper motor neurone damage.</li><li>(B) Hyper-reflexia — Hyper-reflexia suggests organic damage.</li><li>(D) Fasciculations — Fasciculations suggest lower motor neurone damage.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 calls it functional neurological symptom disorder.</li><li>Positive signs of inconsistency are now required, not just ‘no organic cause’.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 73, "question": "<div class=\"ar-block\"><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Assertion (A)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">Including a covariate that is strongly correlated with the dependent variable can increase the power of an ANCOVA relative to ANOVA.</span></div><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Reason (R)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">ANCOVA removes variance in the dependent variable explained by the covariate, reducing the error term.</span></div></div>", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Research, Statistics & Ethics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Both are true, and R explains A. A covariate strongly related to the DV removes part of the variance, shrinking the error term and raising power.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A — B is wrong because R explains A.</li><li>(C) A is true, but R is false — C is wrong because R is true.</li><li>(D) A is false, but R is true — D is wrong because A is true.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ANCOVA assumes homogeneity of regression slopes.</li><li>Covariates should be measured before treatment.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 74, "question": "A 26-year-old woman is brought to the emergency room hours after a suicide attempt following a break-up. She is calm but hopeless and sleeping poorly. In crisis intervention, the therapist&#x27;s FIRST priority is to:", "options": ["Explore childhood attachment experiences", "Begin cognitive restructuring of hopeless beliefs", "Teach long-term problem-solving skills", "Assess lethality and ensure immediate safety"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & Interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Assess lethality and ensure immediate safety</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In crisis intervention, the first step is assessing lethality and ensuring immediate safety. Other work comes after the person is safe.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Explore childhood attachment experiences — Childhood exploration is long-term work.</li><li>(B) Begin cognitive restructuring of hopeless beliefs — Cognitive restructuring follows stabilisation.</li><li>(C) Teach long-term problem-solving skills — Long-term skills come later.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Roberts’ seven-stage model begins with lethality assessment.</li><li>Caplan described crisis theory.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This topic keeps coming back in entrance papers, often with a small change in wording. Revise it well."}, {"id": 75, "question": "On the Rorschach, a record dominated by pure colour (C) responses with few form-dominated responses suggests:", "options": ["Strong cognitive control of affect", "Poor modulation of affect and impulsivity", "High intellectual functioning", "Accurate perceptual reality testing"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Assessment & Psychometrics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Poor modulation of affect and impulsivity</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Pure colour (C) responses — reacting to colour without form — show poor control of emotion and impulsivity. FC &gt; CF &gt; C shows increasing loss of control.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Strong cognitive control of affect — Strong control of affect is shown by FC responses.</li><li>(C) High intellectual functioning — Intelligence is not shown by C responses.</li><li>(D) Accurate perceptual reality testing — Reality testing is shown by form quality.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Rorschach determinants: form, movement, colour, shading.</li><li>Exner’s Comprehensive System (1974).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A favourite of paper-setters — asked again and again in different exams. Expect it in some form."}, {"id": 76, "question": "Which concept in Bowlby&#x27;s attachment theory BEST explains the continuity of attachment style into adult relationships?", "options": ["Zone of proximal development", "Imprinting", "Internal working model", "Social referencing"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Internal working model</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Bowlby’s internal working models are mental pictures of self and others formed in early attachment. They carry expectations into adult relationships.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Zone of proximal development — The ZPD is Vygotsky’s concept.</li><li>(B) Imprinting — Imprinting (Lorenz) is rapid early bonding in birds.</li><li>(D) Social referencing — Social referencing is checking a caregiver’s reactions in uncertain situations.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Hazan and Shaver (1987) applied attachment to adult romantic love.</li><li>The Adult Attachment Interview measures adult attachment.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 77, "question": "Which dementia is typically characterised by early, well-formed visual hallucinations with fluctuating cognition?", "options": ["Alzheimer's disease", "Vascular dementia", "Frontotemporal dementia", "Lewy body dementia"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Lewy body dementia</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Dementia with Lewy bodies: early well-formed visual hallucinations, fluctuating cognition, parkinsonism and REM sleep behaviour disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Alzheimer&#x27;s disease — Alzheimer’s disease starts with memory loss; hallucinations come late.</li><li>(B) Vascular dementia — Vascular dementia shows a stepwise decline.</li><li>(C) Frontotemporal dementia — Frontotemporal dementia starts with behaviour or language change.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>People with DLB react badly to antipsychotics.</li><li>Cholinesterase inhibitors often help.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 78, "question": "In the classic Festinger and Carlsmith study, participants paid $1 for telling another person that a dull task was enjoyable later rated the task more favourably than those paid $20. Which theory was originally proposed to explain this?", "options": ["Cognitive dissonance theory", "Self-perception theory", "Reinforcement theory", "Social comparison theory"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Cognitive dissonance theory</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Festinger and Carlsmith (1959): those paid only $1 had too little justification for lying, so they reduced dissonance by changing their attitude — rating the task as more enjoyable.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Self-perception theory — Self-perception theory (Bem) offered a later alternative explanation.</li><li>(C) Reinforcement theory — Reinforcement theory would predict the $20 group liking it more.</li><li>(D) Social comparison theory — Social comparison theory is about comparing oneself with others.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Festinger proposed dissonance theory in 1957.</li><li>Insufficient justification and effort justification are key dissonance effects.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 79, "question": "A test has a standard deviation of 15 and a reliability coefficient of .91. What is the standard error of measurement?", "options": ["1.35", "2.25", "4.5", "9.0"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Assessment & Psychometrics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>4.5</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> SEM = SD × √(1 − r) = 15 × √(1 − .91) = 15 × √.09 = 15 × 0.3 = 4.5.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) 1.35 — 1.35 is SD × (1 − r), without the square root.</li><li>(B) 2.25 — 2.25 is a calculation error.</li><li>(D) 9.0 — 9.0 is a calculation error.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>95% confidence band = obtained score ± 1.96 × SEM.</li><li>Higher reliability means smaller SEM.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 80, "question": "Arrange the following sleep stages in the order in which they typically appear in the first sleep cycle of a healthy adult: i. N3 (slow-wave sleep) ii. REM sleep iii. N1 iv. N2", "options": ["i – iii – iv – ii", "iii – iv – i – ii", "iii – i – iv – ii", "iv – iii – i – ii"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>iii – iv – i – ii</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In the first sleep cycle: N1 (light, drowsy) → N2 (spindles, K-complexes) → N3 (slow-wave, deep sleep) → REM.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) i – iii – iv – ii — N1 comes first, not N3.</li><li>(C) iii – i – iv – ii — N2 comes before N3.</li><li>(D) iv – iii – i – ii — N1 comes before N2.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>A cycle lasts about 90 minutes.</li><li>REM periods get longer later in the night.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area is asked often. Learn the related points above too."}, {"id": 81, "question": "In a split-brain patient, a picture of a key is flashed briefly to the LEFT visual field only. The patient will MOST likely:", "options": ["Say \"key\" and retrieve it with the right hand", "Say \"key\" but be unable to retrieve it with the left hand", "Report seeing nothing and be unable to pick out the key with either hand", "Report seeing nothing or be unable to name it, yet select the key with the left hand from hidden objects"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Report seeing nothing or be unable to name it, yet select the key with the left hand from hidden objects</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The left visual field goes to the right hemisphere, which cannot speak in most people but controls the left hand. So the patient cannot name the key but can pick it out with the left hand.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Say &quot;key&quot; and retrieve it with the right hand — Naming needs the left hemisphere, which did not see the key.</li><li>(B) Say &quot;key&quot; but be unable to retrieve it with the left hand — The left hand is controlled by the right hemisphere, which did see it.</li><li>(C) Report seeing nothing and be unable to pick out the key with either hand — The right hemisphere can still recognise it and guide the left hand.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Sperry and Gazzaniga did the classic split-brain studies.</li><li>The corpus callosum links the two hemispheres.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a high-frequency area — it has been asked several times in different papers. Keep it at your fingertips."}, {"id": 82, "question": "Which statement about the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act, 2016 (India) is correct?", "options": ["It lists specific learning disability, autism spectrum disorder and mental illness among the specified disabilities", "It excludes mental illness from the specified disabilities", "It is restricted to physical and sensory disabilities", "It recognises only intellectual disability and cerebral palsy"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>It lists specific learning disability, autism spectrum disorder and mental illness among the specified disabilities</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The RPwD Act, 2016 lists 21 specified disabilities, including mental illness, autism spectrum disorder, intellectual disability and specific learning disabilities.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) It excludes mental illness from the specified disabilities — Mental illness is included.</li><li>(C) It is restricted to physical and sensory disabilities — It is not limited to physical and sensory disabilities.</li><li>(D) It recognises only intellectual disability and cerebral palsy — It covers 21 conditions, not just two.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The PwD Act, 1995 recognised 7 disabilities.</li><li>Benchmark disability = at least 40%.</li></ul><p class=\"ex-why\" style=\"margin-top:14px\"><b>Exam note:</b> Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</p>", "examWatch": "This topic keeps coming back in entrance papers, often with a small change in wording. Revise it well."}, {"id": 83, "question": "&quot;Third-wave&quot; cognitive-behavioural therapies include all of the following EXCEPT:", "options": ["Acceptance and Commitment Therapy", "Classical psychoanalysis", "Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy", "Dialectical Behaviour Therapy"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Classical psychoanalysis</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Third-wave CBTs include ACT, DBT and MBCT, with a focus on mindfulness, acceptance and values. Classical psychoanalysis is not a CBT at all.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Acceptance and Commitment Therapy — ACT is a third-wave therapy.</li><li>(C) Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy — MBCT is a third-wave therapy.</li><li>(D) Dialectical Behaviour Therapy — DBT is a third-wave therapy.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>First wave = behaviour therapy; second wave = cognitive therapy.</li><li>Hayes coined the ‘third wave’ label (2004).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A favourite of paper-setters — asked again and again in different exams. Expect it in some form."}, {"id": 84, "question": "In a phenomenological study, the researcher&#x27;s &quot;bracketing&quot; (epoché) is important because it assumes that:", "options": ["The researcher's preconceptions will increase objectivity", "The researcher must set aside prior assumptions to understand participants' lived experience", "The researcher should impose their own interpretation on the data", "The researcher's personal experience is irrelevant to the research"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>The researcher must set aside prior assumptions to understand participants&#x27; lived experience</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Bracketing (epoché, from Husserl) means setting aside one’s own assumptions to describe participants’ lived experience as they experience it.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) The researcher&#x27;s preconceptions will increase objectivity — Preconceptions are set aside, not used.</li><li>(C) The researcher should impose their own interpretation on the data — Imposing interpretations goes against bracketing.</li><li>(D) The researcher&#x27;s personal experience is irrelevant to the research — The researcher’s experience is acknowledged, then set aside — not ignored.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IPA (Smith) is a common phenomenological method in psychology.</li><li>Qualitative rigour includes reflexivity.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 85, "question": "Match the neuropsychological/neuropsychiatric syndromes with their features/localisation:", "options": ["1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d, 5-e", "1-c, 2-b, 3-d, 4-a, 5-e", "1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d, 5-e", "1-c, 2-a, 3-b, 4-d, 5-e"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d, 5-e</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Korsakoff – amnesia and confabulation (mammillary bodies, dorsomedial thalamus); Klüver–Bucy – hyperorality, placidity, hypersexuality (bilateral anterior temporal lobes); Gerstmann – finger agnosia, agraphia, acalculia, left–right confusion (dominant angular gyrus); Bálint – simultanagnosia, optic ataxia, oculomotor apraxia (bilateral parieto-occipital); Capgras – impostor delusion.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) 1-c, 2-b, 3-d, 4-a, 5-e — Option B swaps Gerstmann and Bálint.</li><li>(C) 1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d, 5-e — Option C swaps Korsakoff and Klüver–Bucy.</li><li>(D) 1-c, 2-a, 3-b, 4-d, 5-e — Option D swaps Klüver–Bucy and Gerstmann.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>JEMAS 2024 asked about Klüver–Bucy features.</li><li>Capgras is a delusional misidentification syndrome.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 86, "question": "A 22-year-old reports irresistible daytime sleep attacks, sudden knee buckling when laughing while remaining awake, and vivid hallucinations on falling asleep. Which mechanism is MOST relevant?", "options": ["Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra", "Increased serotonergic tone in the raphe nuclei", "Loss of hypocretin (orexin)-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus", "Hyperactivity of the locus coeruleus"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Loss of hypocretin (orexin)-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Sleep attacks, cataplexy (knee-buckling with laughter) and hypnagogic hallucinations are narcolepsy type 1, caused by loss of hypocretin (orexin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra — Substantia nigra loss causes Parkinson’s disease.</li><li>(B) Increased serotonergic tone in the raphe nuclei — Raised serotonin tone is not the cause.</li><li>(D) Hyperactivity of the locus coeruleus — Locus coeruleus overactivity is not the cause.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Low CSF hypocretin confirms narcolepsy type 1.</li><li>Sleep paralysis is another common feature.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 87, "question": "According to Bandura, the MOST powerful source of self-efficacy is:", "options": ["Vicarious experience", "Verbal persuasion", "Physiological and affective states", "Mastery experiences"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Social, Positive & Indian Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Mastery experiences</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Bandura held that mastery experiences — your own successes — are the strongest source of self-efficacy.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Vicarious experience — Vicarious experience (watching others) is weaker.</li><li>(B) Verbal persuasion — Verbal persuasion is weaker.</li><li>(C) Physiological and affective states — Physiological and emotional states are the weakest.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Self-efficacy is task-specific belief in one’s ability.</li><li>Bandura introduced self-efficacy in 1977.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area is asked often. Learn the related points above too."}, {"id": 88, "question": "A manager who harbours intense, unacknowledged hostility toward a rival colleague becomes unusually gracious and solicitous toward him. Which defence mechanism is operating?", "options": ["Projection", "Reaction formation", "Displacement", "Sublimation"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Reaction formation</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Reaction formation turns an unacceptable feeling into its opposite — hidden hostility shown as excessive kindness.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Projection — Projection would attribute the hostility to the rival.</li><li>(C) Displacement — Displacement would redirect hostility to a safer target.</li><li>(D) Sublimation — Sublimation would channel it into a valued activity.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Reaction formation is a ‘neurotic’ defence in Vaillant’s scheme.</li><li>Anna Freud described defences in 1936.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a high-frequency area — it has been asked several times in different papers. Keep it at your fingertips."}, {"id": 89, "question": "Which of the following neuropsychological batteries was developed in India for use with Indian populations?", "options": ["Halstead–Reitan Battery", "Luria–Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery", "NIMHANS Neuropsychological Battery", "Wechsler Memory Scale"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>NIMHANS Neuropsychological Battery</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The NIMHANS Neuropsychological Battery (Rao, Subbakrishna and Gopukumar, 2004) was developed in India with Indian norms.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Halstead–Reitan Battery — Halstead–Reitan was developed in the USA.</li><li>(B) Luria–Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery — Luria–Nebraska was developed in the USA (Golden).</li><li>(D) Wechsler Memory Scale — The Wechsler Memory Scale was developed in the USA.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction (Pershad and Verma) is another Indian battery.</li><li>The PGI Memory Scale was made by Pershad and Wig (1977).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 90, "question": "(Case 4) A 63-year-old retired bank manager is brought by his wife. Over two years he has made tactless remarks to strangers, started eating sweets compulsively, stopped bathing without being reminded, and shown indifference to his grandchild&#x27;s illness. He is oriented and finds his way around the neighbourhood. His MMSE score is 26/30. MRI shows atrophy of the frontal and anterior temporal lobes. Which neuropsychological profile is MOST expected?", "options": ["Poor verbal fluency, perseveration and impaired set-shifting on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test, with relatively preserved delayed recall", "Marked impairment of delayed free recall with poor recognition and visuoconstructional difficulty", "Fluctuating attention with visual hallucinations and prominent visuospatial deficits", "Stepwise decline with predominant slowing and patchy deficits"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Biological Psychology & Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Poor verbal fluency, perseveration and impaired set-shifting on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test, with relatively preserved delayed recall</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia (disinhibition, apathy, sweet craving, loss of empathy, frontal and temporal atrophy) shows executive problems — poor fluency, perseveration, poor set-shifting — with fairly preserved memory early.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Marked impairment of delayed free recall with poor recognition and visuoconstructional difficulty — Poor recall and recognition with visuospatial problems point to Alzheimer’s disease.</li><li>(C) Fluctuating attention with visual hallucinations and prominent visuospatial deficits — Fluctuations and hallucinations point to Lewy body dementia.</li><li>(D) Stepwise decline with predominant slowing and patchy deficits — Stepwise decline points to vascular dementia.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>MMSE is often near normal early in FTD.</li><li>Arnold Pick described the condition in 1892.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this concept has been asked before in different forms — revise the related points as well."}, {"id": 91, "question": "In a two-parameter logistic (2PL) item response theory model, the item discrimination parameter represents:", "options": ["The probability of guessing the item correctly", "The ability level at which the probability of a correct response is 50%", "The person's latent ability", "The slope of the item characteristic curve, indicating how sharply the item differentiates between people of different ability"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>The slope of the item characteristic curve, indicating how sharply the item differentiates between people of different ability</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In the 2PL model, the discrimination parameter (a) is the slope of the item characteristic curve: a steeper slope separates people of different ability more sharply.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) The probability of guessing the item correctly — Guessing is the c parameter in the 3PL model.</li><li>(B) The ability level at which the probability of a correct response is 50% — The ability level at 50% chance is the difficulty parameter (b).</li><li>(C) The person&#x27;s latent ability — Latent ability is theta (θ), a person parameter.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IMHANS 2021 asked what the ICC slope shows.</li><li>The Rasch model (1PL) fixes discrimination as equal for all items.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This topic keeps coming back in entrance papers, often with a small change in wording. Revise it well."}, {"id": 92, "question": "What is the first-line pharmacological treatment for catatonia?", "options": ["Haloperidol", "Lorazepam", "Lithium", "Clozapine"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Lorazepam</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Benzodiazepines, especially lorazepam, are the first-line treatment for catatonia. ECT is used if they fail.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Haloperidol — Haloperidol can worsen catatonia and raise NMS risk.</li><li>(C) Lithium — Lithium is a mood stabiliser, not a catatonia treatment.</li><li>(D) Clozapine — Clozapine is for treatment-resistant schizophrenia.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The lorazepam challenge test can confirm catatonia.</li><li>The Bush–Francis Catatonia Rating Scale rates severity.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked in earlier papers; examiners like to test it through a case or a NOT question."}, {"id": 93, "question": "A patient is markedly slowed when naming the ink colour of colour words printed in an incongruent colour (e.g. the word &quot;RED&quot; printed in blue). This task primarily taps:", "options": ["Inhibitory control", "Set-shifting", "Working memory updating", "Planning"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Inhibitory control</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Stroop task requires suppressing the automatic urge to read the word in order to name the ink colour — inhibitory control.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Set-shifting — Set-shifting is tested by the WCST or Trail Making B.</li><li>(C) Working memory updating — Working memory updating is tested by n-back tasks.</li><li>(D) Planning — Planning is tested by Tower tasks.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>J. Ridley Stroop described the effect in 1935.</li><li>Miyake’s model: inhibition, shifting, updating.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A favourite of paper-setters — asked again and again in different exams. Expect it in some form."}, {"id": 94, "question": "Which feature is ESSENTIAL to diagnose schizoaffective disorder rather than major depressive disorder with psychotic features?", "options": ["Mood-incongruent delusions", "Psychotic symptoms occurring only during mood episodes", "Delusions or hallucinations for two or more weeks in the absence of a major mood episode", "Presence of Schneiderian first-rank symptoms"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology, Classification & Law", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Delusions or hallucinations for two or more weeks in the absence of a major mood episode</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Schizoaffective disorder needs delusions or hallucinations for at least 2 weeks without a major mood episode, at some point in the illness.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Mood-incongruent delusions — Mood-incongruent delusions can occur in mood disorders too.</li><li>(B) Psychotic symptoms occurring only during mood episodes — Psychosis only during mood episodes points to a mood disorder with psychotic features.</li><li>(D) Presence of Schneiderian first-rank symptoms — First-rank symptoms are not specific.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Mood symptoms must be present for most of the illness.</li><li>Kasanin coined ‘schizoaffective’ (1933).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Questions on this have come before — a quick revision here pays off."}, {"id": 95, "question": "In a one-way repeated-measures ANOVA, Mauchly&#x27;s test of sphericity is significant. The most appropriate action is to:", "options": ["Apply a Bonferroni adjustment to alpha only", "Apply the Greenhouse–Geisser correction to the degrees of freedom", "Switch to an independent-samples ANOVA", "Apply Levene's test"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Research, Statistics & Ethics", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Apply the Greenhouse–Geisser correction to the degrees of freedom</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A significant Mauchly’s test means sphericity is violated. Greenhouse–Geisser (or Huynh–Feldt) corrects the degrees of freedom.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Apply a Bonferroni adjustment to alpha only — Bonferroni corrects for multiple comparisons, not sphericity.</li><li>(C) Switch to an independent-samples ANOVA — The data are repeated measures, so independent ANOVA is wrong.</li><li>(D) Apply Levene&#x27;s test — Levene’s test checks equal variances between groups.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Sphericity = equal variances of the differences between conditions.</li><li>MANOVA is another option when sphericity fails.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 96, "question": "A clinician assesses a 17-year-old for intellectual disability using a test normed 20 years ago. Because of the Flynn effect, the obtained IQ is MOST likely to:", "options": ["Underestimate true ability", "Be unaffected", "Be unreliable but unbiased", "Overestimate ability relative to current norms"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Overestimate ability relative to current norms</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Flynn effect is the rise in IQ scores over generations (about 3 points per decade). Old norms are easier, so scores on old tests overestimate ability compared with current norms.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Underestimate true ability — Old norms inflate, not underestimate, scores.</li><li>(B) Be unaffected — Scores are affected.</li><li>(C) Be unreliable but unbiased — The problem is bias in level, not reliability.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>James Flynn documented this in 1984.</li><li>It matters in ID diagnosis and legal cases.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Seen in past papers more than once. Know the difference between the options well."}, {"id": 97, "question": "According to Gray&#x27;s reinforcement sensitivity theory, high sensitivity of the Behavioural Inhibition System (BIS) predisposes to:", "options": ["Anxiety and anxiety disorders", "Impulsivity and sensation seeking", "Antisocial behaviour driven by reward", "Hypomania"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Core Psychological Processes", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Anxiety and anxiety disorders</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In Gray’s reinforcement sensitivity theory, the Behavioural Inhibition System responds to threat and conflict. A highly sensitive BIS predisposes to anxiety.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(B) Impulsivity and sensation seeking — Impulsivity and sensation seeking link with the Behavioural Approach System.</li><li>(C) Antisocial behaviour driven by reward — Reward-driven antisocial behaviour links with BAS sensitivity and low BIS.</li><li>(D) Hypomania — Hypomania links with BAS overactivity.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Gray revised Eysenck’s theory (1970s).</li><li>The fight-flight-freeze system is the third system.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s analysis, this is a likely new area. Learn the key points above."}, {"id": 98, "question": "Which statement about exposure therapy is MOST consistent with the inhibitory learning model?", "options": ["Extinction erases the original fear association", "Within-session habituation is necessary for long-term success", "Extinction creates new inhibitory learning that competes with the original fear memory, and violating expectancies and varying contexts strengthens it", "Safety behaviours should be encouraged to make exposure tolerable"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & Interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Extinction creates new inhibitory learning that competes with the original fear memory, and violating expectancies and varying contexts strengthens it</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Craske’s inhibitory learning model says exposure creates new safety learning that competes with the old fear memory. Violating expectations and varying contexts strengthen this new learning.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Extinction erases the original fear association — Extinction does not erase the original fear memory.</li><li>(B) Within-session habituation is necessary for long-term success — Within-session habituation is not needed for long-term success.</li><li>(D) Safety behaviours should be encouraged to make exposure tolerable — Safety behaviours weaken exposure.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Relapse can happen through renewal, reinstatement and spontaneous recovery.</li><li>Older models stressed habituation (Foa and Kozak’s emotional processing).</li></ul>", "examWatch": "Asked several times across papers. The questions are usually direct, so these are easy marks if you revise."}, {"id": 99, "question": "The Social Readjustment Rating Scale (Holmes and Rahe) is used to measure:", "options": ["Daily hassles", "Life events and their potential impact on health", "Personality traits", "Social support networks"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "General Clinical Psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>Life events and their potential impact on health</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Social Readjustment Rating Scale (Holmes and Rahe, 1967) gives ‘life change units’ to major life events. High totals predict illness.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Daily hassles — Daily hassles are measured by Kanner and Lazarus’s Hassles Scale.</li><li>(C) Personality traits — Personality traits are not measured by it.</li><li>(D) Social support networks — Social support is measured by other scales.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Death of a spouse = 100 units; divorce = 73.</li><li>IHBAS 2025 asked what the SRRS focuses on.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This area has appeared in previous papers. It may well come in your exam too."}, {"id": 100, "question": "A 4-year-old adopted at age 3 after institutional care with severe early deprivation rarely seeks comfort when distressed, shows limited positive affect, and does not respond to comfort. The parents wonder about autism. Which finding BEST favours reactive attachment disorder over autism spectrum disorder?", "options": ["Delayed speech with echolalia", "Stereotyped hand movements and insistence on sameness", "Poor eye contact and limited reciprocal play", "A documented history of pathogenic care, absence of restricted/repetitive behaviours, and improved social responsiveness with a consistent caregiver"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Development, Child & Adolescent", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p>A documented history of pathogenic care, absence of restricted/repetitive behaviours, and improved social responsiveness with a consistent caregiver</p><p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Reactive attachment disorder needs a history of severe neglect (pathogenic care), with no restricted or repetitive behaviours. Social responsiveness improves with a consistent caregiver.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>(A) Delayed speech with echolalia — Echolalia and delayed speech point to autism.</li><li>(B) Stereotyped hand movements and insistence on sameness — Stereotypies and sameness point to autism.</li><li>(C) Poor eye contact and limited reciprocal play — Poor eye contact and limited play can occur in both.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RAD is in the trauma- and stressor-related disorders chapter.</li><li>Disinhibited social engagement disorder is its counterpart.</li></ul><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Column I|Column II</li><li>1. BPRS|a. Severity of obsessions and compulsions</li><li>2. YMRS|b. Severity of manic symptoms</li><li>3. Y-BOCS|c. Disability associated with mental illness (India)</li><li>4. IDEAS|d. Broad-spectrum psychopathology severity</li><li>5. HAM-D|e. Severity of depressive symptoms</li><li>Column I|Column II</li><li>1. Cronbach&#x27;s alpha|a. Agreement between raters corrected for chance</li><li>2. Cohen&#x27;s kappa|b. Internal consistency for items scored on a continuous or polytomous scale</li><li>3. KR-20|c. Internal consistency for dichotomously scored items</li><li>4. Spearman–Brown formula|d. Estimating reliability of a full-length test from half-test correlation</li><li>5. Test–retest correlation|e. Temporal stability of scores</li><li>Column I|Column II</li><li>1. Derailment|a. Sudden interruption of the train of thought, leaving a blank</li><li>2. Tangentiality|b. Associations governed by sound rather than meaning</li><li>3. Circumstantiality|c. Slippage between unrelated topics with loosened associations</li><li>4. Thought blocking|d. Oblique or irrelevant replies that never reach the point</li><li>5. Clang association|e. Excess detail and delay, but the goal is eventually reached</li><li>Column I|Column II</li><li>1. Wise mind|a. Logotherapy</li><li>2. Downward arrow|b. Gestalt therapy</li><li>3. Empty chair|c. Dialectical behaviour therapy</li><li>4. Unconditional positive regard|d. Person-centred therapy</li><li>5. Paradoxical intention|e. Cognitive therapy</li><li>Column I|Column II</li><li>1. Korsakoff syndrome|a. Finger agnosia, agraphia, acalculia, left–right disorientation (dominant angular gyrus)</li><li>2. Klüver–Bucy syndrome|b. Hyperorality, placidity, hypersexuality (bilateral anterior temporal/amygdala damage)</li><li>3. Gerstmann syndrome|c. Anterograde amnesia and confabulation (mammillary bodies/dorsomedial thalamus)</li><li>4. Bálint syndrome|d. Simultanagnosia, optic ataxia, oculomotor apraxia (bilateral parieto-occipital damage)</li><li>5. Capgras syndrome|e. Delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an identical impostor</li></ul>", "examWatch": "A fresh, predicted question. The neighbouring topics are asked often, so it is worth knowing."}];
